    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d400">

                <pb facs="0505" n="495" xml:id="pg495"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.796/926b: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Australia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-06-21T19:00:00-04:00">June 21, 1944—7
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>76. A letter dated June 5 written by Prime Minister Curtin while in Washington
                    referred to our invitation for bilateral aviation discussions. You were advised
                    of this in the Department’s telegram 61, May 10, 10 p.m.<anchor n="38"
                        xml:id="d400-f1"/> Mr. Curtin’s letter refers to his interim reply of May 13
                    and confirms that arrangements for Australia to participate in talks at the
                    present time would not be practicable. However, he promised to look into the
                    matter immediately on his return to Australia and to advise further.</p>
                <p>On a suitable occasion please endeavor to ascertain if Australia will be able to
                    enter into such conversations with us in Washington and the approximate
                    date.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
